      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the total area of the shaded regions in Wei's logo, we need to calculate the area of the square and the total area of the circles inscribed within it. Here are the steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the side length of the square:**\n   The side length of the square is given as 20 inches.\n\n2. **Calculate the area of the square:**\n   The area \\( A_{\\text{square}} \\) of a square is given by the formula:\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{square}} = \\text{side length}^2\n   \\]\n   Substituting the given side length:\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{square}} = 20^2 = 400 \\text{ square inches}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the radius of each inscribed circle:**\n   Each circle is tangent to two sides of the square and its two adjacent circles. This means the diameter of each circle is equal to the side length of the smaller square formed by the centers of the four circles. The smaller square's side length is equal to the distance between the centers of two adjacent circles, which is equal to the side length of the square minus twice the radius of the circle. Let \\( r \\) be the radius of each circle. Then the side length of the smaller square is \\( 2r \\).\n\n   The side length of the original square is 20 inches, so:\n   \\[\n   20 = 2r + 2r = 4r\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\( r \\):\n   \\[\n   r = \\frac{20}{4} = 5 \\text{ inches}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the area of one circle:**\n   The area \\( A_{\\text{circle}} \\) of a circle is given by the formula:\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{circle}} = \\pi r^2\n   \\]\n   Substituting the radius \\( r = 5 \\) inches:\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{circle}} = \\pi (5)^2 = 25\\pi \\text{ square inches}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the total area of the four circles:**\n   Since there are four circles, the total area \\( A_{\\text{total circles}} \\) of the circles is:\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{total circles}} = 4 \\times 25\\pi = 100\\pi \\text{ square inches}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Calculate the shaded area:**\n   The shaded area is the area of the square minus the total area of the circles:\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{shaded}} = A_{\\text{square}} - A_{\\text{total circles}} = 400 - 100\\pi\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of the shaded regions is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{400 - 100\\pi}\n\\]",
